    "score": 26.959395255006115,
    "snippet": "---\ntitle: clan of xymox\nauthor: Stephen Kennedy\ndate: 2017-03-31\n---\nLuminous Dash BE by Didier Becu\n\nLuminous Dash (BE) review by Didier Becu\n\nSadly not everyone is aware of it, but in 1981 a band was formed in the Netherlands that would leave an indelible mark on both the post-punk and goth music. Their name: Clan Of Xymox. Discovered at the time by Brendan Perry of Dead Can Dance which resulted in a record deal on 4AD so they were then on the same label with leading and influential bands like Cocteau Twins and later Pixies. Nowadays residing in Germany and with a different line-up, but carrying on for more than 40 years. Not like so many others do by tapping into the past, but by constantly releasing new albums that would make contemporary 'post-punk bands' blush.\n\nOver the years, Ronny Moorings' band made it their trademark to use an amalgam of influences \u2013 goth, synthpop, industrial, postpunk and even a lot of David Bowie \u2013 to create a sound that perfectly fits the world as it is today. Clan Of Xymox's music only got darker. On their latest LP Exodus, themes of war and the ever more imminent nuclear war are not avoided. Themes that do not make you happy, but Clan Of Xymox succeeds time and again in turning these topics into melodic songs that inevitably make you dance. Therefore Clan Of Xymox feels at times like the Titanic's house orchestra, which keeps on playing. Dancing to your own demise.\n\nBlood Of Christ is their latest release. 14 songs that actually consist of two tracks: Blood Of Christ, which we already know from the Exodus album, and You're The One, which we hear for the first time. Two songs that deserve the term \"Xymox classic\". A cartload of remixes is usually not a good sign and threatens to get boring, but not if you're Clan Of Xymox!\n\nNo extended mixes that simply last a minute or two longer than the original, but both songs are treated from a different musical perspective. This sometimes results in a more industrial sound and other times more like, say, Depeche Mode. Not that we should use other names as references. Clan Of Xymox has been Clan Of Xymox for forty years. For which kudos!\n\nDevolution Magazine\n\nBlood of Christ EP\n\n"
  },
