    "snippet": "---\ntitle: American Poetry | History, Timeline & Famous Poets\ndate: 2023-03-10\n---\nMargaret has taught both college and high school English and has a master's degree in English from Mississippi State University. She holds a Mississippi AA Educator License.\n\nAmerican Poetry | History, Timeline & Famous Poets\n\nTable of Contents\n\n- What is American Poetry?\n\n- American Poets of the Colonial Period\n\n- American Poets of the 18th and 19th Centuries\n\n- 20th-Century American Poets and Poetry\n\n- 21st-Century American Poets\n\n- Timeline of American Poetry\n\n- Lesson Summary\n\nAmerican poetry is poetry written in the period from the 17th century to the present. It originated in what would eventually become the United States; however, literature existed in the Americas for thousands of years before English-speaking European settlers began establishing colonies in the early 17th century.\n\nFrom the earliest of poems until the 21st century, American poets have used this literary genre to explore themes of universal interest such as personal, national, and spiritual identity, love, death, relationships, and other themes that address the human condition.\n\nAmerican poems of the 17th century typically reflect Puritan spirituality and society. In the 18th century, many poems focused on burgeoning personal and national identity concerns. Many well-regarded Americans also wrote poetry in the 19th century. The themes of these works are as varied as their authors, but some of the most influential poets of the period examine grief, nature, and the illusion of separateness. American poets of the 20th century often consider all of these themes: justice, war, racism, and cultural identity. Early 21st-century poetry typically offers realistic portraits of people, places, and events.\n\nTo unlock this lesson you must be a Study.com Member.\n\nCreate your account\n\nThe American colonial era occurred from 1607\u20131775. Native American people produced oral literature, including poetry, before this period. There is no extant Native American poetry, however, because Native American people in North America did not have a writing system before contact with European settlers in the 17th century.\n\nIn the early years of the period, Puritan influence was strong. When European colonists arrived in the New World with a printing press, ministers decided after some deliberation that the first document printed on the press should translate the biblical Psalms. This first book published in the colonies, and the first book of"
