    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Bill Manhire's Poetry: Some Personal Responses\ndate: 2000-02-05\n---\nReturn to No Frills NZ Literature home page.\n\nIn 2000, when I reviewed Bill Manhire's collection of essays and interviews, Doubtful Sounds, for the New Zealand Listener, I was struck by the curious discrepancy between Manhire's public persona and his poetry.[1] In his interviews and essays Manhire seemed congenial and also confident about his work--at times even insouciant--while his poems themselves are famously retiring, hesitant and infused with melancholy. Above all, it seemed remarkable to me that a writer of such difficult verse should be viewed in New Zealand as an accessible and even as a beloved literary figure.[2] No doubt it is naive to assume that a writer's oeuvre is nothing more than an extension of his personality, and no one would want to complain if Manhire's apparent clubbability has broadened his readership. His work can stand on its merits. However, as others before me have noted, critics have sometimes been reluctant to engage with Manhire's poetry, as if accepting that the spotlight of analysis might ruin its delicate effects.[3] And perhaps Manhire's public attitude to literature has been a contributing factor in this, such as his insistence that 'I don't like that high cultural view of poetry at all, where it becomes a vehicle whereby people offer their superior wisdom to the world'.[4]\n\nManhire has been at pains to imply that his poems are the mysterious results of humorous, good-natured bricolage.[5] He describes 'Allen Curnow Meets Judge Dredd', for example, as 'an affectionate tease'.[6] He suggests that the charm of 'Magasin' is that it contains 'a range of unlikely things' and 'ends with a disjointing, code-switching joke'.[7] He likes to define his poetry as 'strongly marked by tonal drifts and lurches'.[8] But it is possible for a reader to be too diffident in the face of a poet's claims to mystery. In their very difficulty Manhire's poems certainly require critical reading, even if such an approach must be smuggled in by means of the cuddlier labels--the reader's imaginative participation, or a poet-to-reader conversation--that Manhire is fond of using.[9] Thus, partly because I wish to contradict some of Manhire's public claims, and partly because"
