    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Review of Almost Human by Thomas Centolella + an interview with the author\ndate: 2023-01-01\n---\nReview of Almost Human by Thomas Centolella + an interview with the author\n\nPaperback: 82 pages\n\nPublisher: Tupelo Press, 2017\n\nPurchase @ Tupelo Press\n\nReview by Margaret Stawowy.\n\nEdward Hirsch awarded Tupelo Press's Dorset Prize to Thomas Centolella's fourth book of poetry, Almost Human. The volume begins with a quote attributed to Pierre Teilhard de Chardin: \"We are not human beings having a spiritual experience. We are spiritual beings having a human experience.\"\n\nIn this collection, the reader is airdropped into poems inhabited by a variety of beings, some grounded in human form, others speaking from a removed, disembodied viewpoint, as if from another realm. Yet all speak to the confounding situation of having to live as flawed creatures in the physical world with all-too-needy bodies. \"To look and not desire, that's the trick.\" --A trick that is difficult to master. In \"Ave Maria\":\n\nHave you ever loved someone deeply, but were allowed to do so\n\nonly in scarce moments, though each moment tried its best\n\nto convince you it was made to last, until\n\nagainst your better judgment you came to believe it?\n\nLove and longing follow patterns of discovery and ecstatic connection, followed by fracture and aloneness. Though there is often despair, these poems explore and push beyond physical laws into the metaphysical. In \"Vermeer: Woman in Blue Reading a Letter,\" the narrator invites the reader to \"float\" into the Rijksmusuem and to merge with a Vermeer painting. The woman in blue is in her last trimester of pregnancy and the description of the expression on her face tells the reader that the news in the letter is not good: \"the trembling of her young lip. . ./It's your lip, isn't it?\" Centuries fall away:\n\n. . .And the sun\n\nthat stuns her room, floods your own.\n\nThe same sun. On the wall behind her,\n\na map of the Old World. And on both\n\nyour faces, a map of the new one.\n\nCentolella crafts elliptical, lustrous language, rich with assonance and consonance and with subtle rhyme, both auditory and visual. There is an ethereal quality to the voices in these poems. They speak with authority, with a kind of resigned acceptance"
  },
